WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.747 --> 00:00:04.330
CAROLYN UJCIC: Welcome
to Cloud OnAir,

00:00:04.330 --> 00:00:06.700
live webinars from Google Cloud.

00:00:06.700 --> 00:00:09.310
We're hosting these
webinars every Tuesday.

00:00:09.310 --> 00:00:10.966
My name is Carolyn Ujcic.

00:00:10.966 --> 00:00:12.340
And today, I'll
be talking to you

00:00:12.340 --> 00:00:14.770
about how to start ML projects.

00:00:14.770 --> 00:00:17.840
You can ask questions
any time on the platform,

00:00:17.840 --> 00:00:20.831
and we have Googlers on
standby to answer them.

00:00:20.831 --> 00:00:21.580
Let's get started.

00:00:25.190 --> 00:00:27.610
So as I mentioned,
this presentation today

00:00:27.610 --> 00:00:29.920
is around how to start
machine learning projects.

00:00:29.920 --> 00:00:32.710
And it's called
"from hype to value."

00:00:32.710 --> 00:00:35.140
Machine learning is a
big buzzword these days,

00:00:35.140 --> 00:00:37.540
but how do you know
if you're selecting

00:00:37.540 --> 00:00:40.750
the right project that will
justify your investment?

00:00:40.750 --> 00:00:43.700
We'll tell you more about
how to do that today.

00:00:43.700 --> 00:00:46.900
So our discussion for today
is going to just start around

00:00:46.900 --> 00:00:48.040
the ML journey--

00:00:48.040 --> 00:00:49.210
what it looks like.

00:00:49.210 --> 00:00:53.060
What is that typical path
for companies like you?

00:00:53.060 --> 00:00:55.610
Then, we'll talk about
use case identification

00:00:55.610 --> 00:00:59.030
and qualification, which is an
important step in making sure

00:00:59.030 --> 00:01:02.210
that you select a feasible
machine learning project.

00:01:02.210 --> 00:01:04.220
We'll walk through
a few case studies.

00:01:04.220 --> 00:01:05.930
And then at the end,
we'll have more time

00:01:05.930 --> 00:01:09.710
for a question and answer.

00:01:09.710 --> 00:01:13.190
So first, I want to
start by debunking

00:01:13.190 --> 00:01:15.350
a bit of a myth about
machine learning.

00:01:15.350 --> 00:01:17.760
I often hear this
from customers.

00:01:17.760 --> 00:01:20.030
So you have some data.

00:01:20.030 --> 00:01:21.350
That's your input.

00:01:21.350 --> 00:01:23.870
You feed it into a
machine learning model.

00:01:23.870 --> 00:01:26.360
That performs some
magic, and then you

00:01:26.360 --> 00:01:28.910
get your answers,
or your output.

00:01:28.910 --> 00:01:31.580
That's not actually
quite how it works.

00:01:31.580 --> 00:01:33.990
So when you think
about machine learning,

00:01:33.990 --> 00:01:36.530
you can replace the word
"magic" with "math."

00:01:36.530 --> 00:01:38.840
So machine learning
fundamentally

00:01:38.840 --> 00:01:44.400
is using math to
make predictions.

00:01:44.400 --> 00:01:47.240
So let's talk a little bit
more about exactly what machine

00:01:47.240 --> 00:01:48.440
learning is.

00:01:48.440 --> 00:01:50.210
The way I think about
it is it's a way

00:01:50.210 --> 00:01:53.270
to maximize the data
you already have.

00:01:53.270 --> 00:01:56.000
And you're probably already
using it, or at least

00:01:56.000 --> 00:01:58.020
collecting a lot of data.

00:01:58.020 --> 00:02:01.130
So think about machine
learning in these three steps.

00:02:01.130 --> 00:02:03.890
You have a large
quantity of data.

00:02:03.890 --> 00:02:07.100
You apply a mathematical
algorithm to that data.

00:02:07.100 --> 00:02:09.650
And then you get a
forward-looking prediction that

00:02:09.650 --> 00:02:12.140
will help you make a decision.

00:02:12.140 --> 00:02:15.650
Now, I want you to keep in
mind these three steps as you

00:02:15.650 --> 00:02:19.710
think about how to apply machine
learning in your organization.

00:02:19.710 --> 00:02:22.950
Sometimes, machine learning is
not always the best solution.

00:02:22.950 --> 00:02:24.800
But when you're making
repeated decisions

00:02:24.800 --> 00:02:26.760
and you have large
quantity of data,

00:02:26.760 --> 00:02:29.150
then it can often be
a really great way

00:02:29.150 --> 00:02:33.100
to get a competitive advantage.

00:02:33.100 --> 00:02:35.820
So within Google Cloud, we
have a professional services

00:02:35.820 --> 00:02:40.500
organization that helps a lot
of customers discover feasible,

00:02:40.500 --> 00:02:44.190
high-impact ML use cases
for their companies.

00:02:44.190 --> 00:02:48.090
And we really think about
this as involving these three

00:02:48.090 --> 00:02:49.440
important parts.

00:02:49.440 --> 00:02:51.960
First, is we want to
work closely with you

00:02:51.960 --> 00:02:53.730
and your domain experts.

00:02:53.730 --> 00:02:55.530
You know your business best.

00:02:55.530 --> 00:02:58.140
And we're basically
here to guide you.

00:02:58.140 --> 00:03:00.030
And this presentation
as well will hopefully

00:03:00.030 --> 00:03:02.070
guide you as you're
thinking about how to start

00:03:02.070 --> 00:03:04.330
ML in your organization.

00:03:04.330 --> 00:03:07.000
The next step is
feasible, not magic.

00:03:07.000 --> 00:03:10.770
So again, the fact that
machine learning is magic--

00:03:10.770 --> 00:03:11.940
it's a bit of a myth.

00:03:11.940 --> 00:03:15.000
So we really want to help you
qualify your machine learning

00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:17.880
use cases and look at if
they're actually technically

00:03:17.880 --> 00:03:20.670
feasible for your
organization based on the data

00:03:20.670 --> 00:03:22.390
that you have.

00:03:22.390 --> 00:03:24.100
The last part
involves making sure

00:03:24.100 --> 00:03:26.950
that those use cases
are high impact--

00:03:26.950 --> 00:03:29.470
so looking at things
like what sort of revenue

00:03:29.470 --> 00:03:32.350
might be generated by this
machine learning use case.

00:03:32.350 --> 00:03:34.840
Or what sort of
cost savings can we

00:03:34.840 --> 00:03:38.400
expect if we were to complete
this machine learning project?

00:03:42.380 --> 00:03:44.900
So we think about the
machine learning lifecycle

00:03:44.900 --> 00:03:49.340
as having these 10 steps, and
it's an iterative process.

00:03:49.340 --> 00:03:52.460
Today, we're going to really
focus on steps one and two.

00:03:52.460 --> 00:03:55.080
But I'll walk you through
the end-to-end life cycle

00:03:55.080 --> 00:03:57.830
so you have a sense of
what comes after you've

00:03:57.830 --> 00:04:00.270
defined those ML use cases.

00:04:00.270 --> 00:04:03.200
So, as I mentioned, today
we're focusing on ML use case

00:04:03.200 --> 00:04:04.156
identification.

00:04:04.156 --> 00:04:06.530
And that's the best place to
start for a machine learning

00:04:06.530 --> 00:04:07.610
project.

00:04:07.610 --> 00:04:11.360
You want to get very specific in
terms of what you're predicting

00:04:11.360 --> 00:04:13.440
and what decisions
you're going to make.

00:04:13.440 --> 00:04:15.380
So, for example, if
you're forecasting

00:04:15.380 --> 00:04:19.130
sales for your
stores, you want to be

00:04:19.130 --> 00:04:22.340
sure about exactly
what you're predicting.

00:04:22.340 --> 00:04:27.710
Are you predicting sales for
each of your SKUs every week

00:04:27.710 --> 00:04:32.280
or for the next 12 weeks
or for the next 52 weeks?

00:04:32.280 --> 00:04:34.160
So you need to get
very specific in terms

00:04:34.160 --> 00:04:36.500
of what you want to predict.

00:04:36.500 --> 00:04:39.050
The next step involves
data exploration.

00:04:39.050 --> 00:04:41.690
So during this step,
your data analyst

00:04:41.690 --> 00:04:44.480
will perform exploratory
data analysis

00:04:44.480 --> 00:04:46.580
to really understand your data.

00:04:46.580 --> 00:04:48.140
What do you have?

00:04:48.140 --> 00:04:50.440
Is it good quality?

00:04:50.440 --> 00:04:53.880
How can we link together
your different data sets

00:04:53.880 --> 00:04:57.700
so that we can use them in
the machine learning project?

00:04:57.700 --> 00:05:00.720
We'll also try to look at
relationships between the data

00:05:00.720 --> 00:05:03.445
as well as any signals
that might be missing

00:05:03.445 --> 00:05:05.820
and how we might be able to
bring in some additional data

00:05:05.820 --> 00:05:08.340
sets so we can
have those signals

00:05:08.340 --> 00:05:11.920
and capture them in our
machine learning model.

00:05:11.920 --> 00:05:15.050
Step three is around
selecting an algorithm.

00:05:15.050 --> 00:05:18.550
And this often involves
research in the problem space

00:05:18.550 --> 00:05:19.790
that you're working in.

00:05:19.790 --> 00:05:23.260
So, for example, with that
sales forecasting idea,

00:05:23.260 --> 00:05:25.630
you would want to do
some review of what

00:05:25.630 --> 00:05:30.220
are some other successful
ML applications related

00:05:30.220 --> 00:05:31.570
to sales forecasting.

00:05:31.570 --> 00:05:33.910
What sort of models
are people using?

00:05:33.910 --> 00:05:35.740
And there's a wealth
of information

00:05:35.740 --> 00:05:38.560
out there that you can really
pull from so that you'll

00:05:38.560 --> 00:05:40.710
know where to start.

00:05:40.710 --> 00:05:43.260
After that, you get into your
data pipeline and feature

00:05:43.260 --> 00:05:44.760
engineering.

00:05:44.760 --> 00:05:46.770
During this step
is when you really

00:05:46.770 --> 00:05:48.690
think about what are
those inputs that

00:05:48.690 --> 00:05:52.000
are going into our
machine learning bottle.

00:05:52.000 --> 00:05:53.922
So when you think
about those inputs,

00:05:53.922 --> 00:05:55.380
you'll want to
consider, again, how

00:05:55.380 --> 00:05:58.450
can you link them together into
a single record that will sort

00:05:58.450 --> 00:06:01.270
of feed into that ML model?

00:06:01.270 --> 00:06:02.860
You'll also want
to think about what

00:06:02.860 --> 00:06:05.020
are the components of
that data pipeline.

00:06:05.020 --> 00:06:06.830
And we call those features.

00:06:06.830 --> 00:06:10.400
So, again, just think of those
as the inputs to the ML model.

00:06:10.400 --> 00:06:12.830
But you can get
creative around how

00:06:12.830 --> 00:06:15.560
you engineer those features.

00:06:15.560 --> 00:06:20.770
So, for example, you might look
at the previous week's sales

00:06:20.770 --> 00:06:23.170
for that sales
forecasting use case.

00:06:23.170 --> 00:06:25.990
But maybe you'll also
look at same-store sales

00:06:25.990 --> 00:06:32.320
from one year ago or maybe
sales in that geographic region

00:06:32.320 --> 00:06:34.450
two years ago.

00:06:34.450 --> 00:06:36.300
This is where you
can also start to be

00:06:36.300 --> 00:06:39.032
creative with your external
data sets as well--

00:06:39.032 --> 00:06:39.990
so things like weather.

00:06:39.990 --> 00:06:42.281
You could pull in the average
temperature from the week

00:06:42.281 --> 00:06:43.230
before.

00:06:43.230 --> 00:06:46.320
Or if there were any
inclement weather conditions,

00:06:46.320 --> 00:06:51.220
like a flood or a tornado,
that may impact sales as well.

00:06:51.220 --> 00:06:54.690
Following that, you
code your ML model.

00:06:54.690 --> 00:06:56.370
So this is where
your ML engineer

00:06:56.370 --> 00:06:58.980
will come in and actually
create that machine learning

00:06:58.980 --> 00:07:02.040
model that will be
making the predictions.

00:07:02.040 --> 00:07:04.430
And often, this is a
very iterative process.

00:07:04.430 --> 00:07:06.900
And that's why you see
step six listed here.

00:07:06.900 --> 00:07:09.120
You actually go back and repeat.

00:07:09.120 --> 00:07:12.060
You may tweak your data
pipeline and your features

00:07:12.060 --> 00:07:14.100
and then see how
your model performs.

00:07:14.100 --> 00:07:17.400
And then you may try it again
with some different features.

00:07:17.400 --> 00:07:19.140
Or you may remove
some of the features

00:07:19.140 --> 00:07:22.500
that you thought were important,
but maybe they aren't really

00:07:22.500 --> 00:07:24.160
improving your ML model.

00:07:24.160 --> 00:07:25.920
So again, keep in
mind that you're

00:07:25.920 --> 00:07:29.610
going to iterate over and
over again on your ML model

00:07:29.610 --> 00:07:32.490
until you get some results that
you think are meaningful to you

00:07:32.490 --> 00:07:33.660
and your business.

00:07:33.660 --> 00:07:35.140
And that's what follows--

00:07:35.140 --> 00:07:36.570
step seven.

00:07:36.570 --> 00:07:38.460
During this step,
it's important for you

00:07:38.460 --> 00:07:41.250
to share those preliminary
results with your business

00:07:41.250 --> 00:07:44.850
stakeholders so they can
really see what you've learned.

00:07:44.850 --> 00:07:48.450
Oftentimes, we'll define
specific ML hypotheses

00:07:48.450 --> 00:07:50.230
during an ML project.

00:07:50.230 --> 00:07:51.900
And this is a
really exciting part

00:07:51.900 --> 00:07:53.809
of the project
where you can share

00:07:53.809 --> 00:07:55.850
if you proved those
hypotheses with your business

00:07:55.850 --> 00:07:57.100
stakeholders.

00:07:57.100 --> 00:07:59.340
So, for example, maybe
one of your hypotheses

00:07:59.340 --> 00:08:04.660
were around weather has
an influence on our sales.

00:08:04.660 --> 00:08:06.940
So at this point, you
probably have some data

00:08:06.940 --> 00:08:10.780
that you can show to
prove or at least show

00:08:10.780 --> 00:08:13.210
some preliminary
findings related

00:08:13.210 --> 00:08:16.010
to some of those hypotheses.

00:08:16.010 --> 00:08:18.980
Once you've gotten to
a level of success that

00:08:18.980 --> 00:08:20.660
meets your business
needs, you can

00:08:20.660 --> 00:08:22.490
start to plan for deployment.

00:08:22.490 --> 00:08:24.200
And this step often
involves thinking

00:08:24.200 --> 00:08:26.750
about the end-to-end
business process that's

00:08:26.750 --> 00:08:28.940
affected by this ML model.

00:08:28.940 --> 00:08:31.700
So before, we were just trying
to get those predictions right.

00:08:31.700 --> 00:08:35.100
But now, we need to think
about system integration.

00:08:35.100 --> 00:08:38.659
So is this going to be
part of maybe a forecasting

00:08:38.659 --> 00:08:40.789
platform that you use today?

00:08:40.789 --> 00:08:43.789
Does this need to be
integrated with some dashboard

00:08:43.789 --> 00:08:46.220
that your executives look at?

00:08:46.220 --> 00:08:49.510
So you'll need to come up with
a plan and the requirements

00:08:49.510 --> 00:08:51.170
required for deployment.

00:08:51.170 --> 00:08:54.200
After that, you actually
operationalize your model

00:08:54.200 --> 00:08:55.160
into production.

00:08:55.160 --> 00:08:57.450
And Google has a lot of
tools to help you do that.

00:08:57.450 --> 00:08:59.330
And then you want to
monitor your model.

00:08:59.330 --> 00:09:02.510
So over time, your model
actually needs to be retrained.

00:09:02.510 --> 00:09:06.110
You actually need to continue
to teach it using new data,

00:09:06.110 --> 00:09:08.120
because we all know
your business is going

00:09:08.120 --> 00:09:10.070
to continue to grow and change.

00:09:10.070 --> 00:09:11.720
So during the
monitor phase, you'll

00:09:11.720 --> 00:09:13.490
want to come up with
some specific metrics

00:09:13.490 --> 00:09:15.890
that you look at so you
can determine when you want

00:09:15.890 --> 00:09:18.590
to do that retraining
or when you want

00:09:18.590 --> 00:09:21.660
to maybe create a new ML model.

00:09:21.660 --> 00:09:23.750
So monitoring those
metrics will be

00:09:23.750 --> 00:09:27.170
important to know when should
you start that retraining

00:09:27.170 --> 00:09:30.590
process or when should you maybe
start development on another ML

00:09:30.590 --> 00:09:31.592
model.

00:09:31.592 --> 00:09:33.800
So that's the end-to-end
machine learning life cycle.

00:09:33.800 --> 00:09:36.830
Again, today, we're going to be
focused on steps one and two.

00:09:36.830 --> 00:09:38.540
But hopefully, this
gives you a taste

00:09:38.540 --> 00:09:40.370
of what the entire
process looks like.

00:09:43.520 --> 00:09:45.020
So now, you might
be thinking, OK.

00:09:45.020 --> 00:09:46.960
This sounds great,
but how do I do this?

00:09:46.960 --> 00:09:48.820
Who needs to be involved?

00:09:48.820 --> 00:09:51.680
Well, machine learning
is really a team sport.

00:09:51.680 --> 00:09:54.940
It doesn't just involve that ML
engineer that we talked about.

00:09:54.940 --> 00:09:56.650
There's actually a
variety of players

00:09:56.650 --> 00:09:58.690
that you want to make
sure are engaged.

00:09:58.690 --> 00:10:00.630
So these folks come
from different areas

00:10:00.630 --> 00:10:04.630
of your business-- engineering,
analytics and science, and also

00:10:04.630 --> 00:10:06.040
domain experts.

00:10:06.040 --> 00:10:08.710
I'm going to talk a little bit
about some of the typical roles

00:10:08.710 --> 00:10:11.570
that we see involved in these
machine learning projects.

00:10:11.570 --> 00:10:12.979
And I want you to
think about who

00:10:12.979 --> 00:10:14.770
you might be able to
bring into these roles

00:10:14.770 --> 00:10:16.930
in your organization.

00:10:16.930 --> 00:10:20.370
So the first is your project
sponsor or decision maker.

00:10:20.370 --> 00:10:22.610
This is usually
a leader that has

00:10:22.610 --> 00:10:24.860
some ownership of
the process that's

00:10:24.860 --> 00:10:28.940
going to be impacted by ML,
someone who can really come up

00:10:28.940 --> 00:10:32.570
with the success
criteria for the project

00:10:32.570 --> 00:10:35.510
and can also decide
when something might be

00:10:35.510 --> 00:10:38.470
ready to move into production.

00:10:38.470 --> 00:10:40.780
The next person is
a domain expert.

00:10:40.780 --> 00:10:45.360
So this is usually someone
who's doing the process today.

00:10:45.360 --> 00:10:47.820
And this is someone
who is really involved

00:10:47.820 --> 00:10:50.010
in perhaps making a
lot of human decisions

00:10:50.010 --> 00:10:51.600
around the ML problem.

00:10:51.600 --> 00:10:53.370
So for that sales
forecasting example,

00:10:53.370 --> 00:10:55.740
it could be one of
your planners or one

00:10:55.740 --> 00:10:57.900
of your financial
analysts who's actually

00:10:57.900 --> 00:10:59.822
doing the forecasting today.

00:10:59.822 --> 00:11:01.530
And we'd want to engage
with that person.

00:11:01.530 --> 00:11:04.200
Understand, how are they
making their decision?

00:11:04.200 --> 00:11:08.040
And how can we try to
get an ML model to think

00:11:08.040 --> 00:11:10.480
about the problem in that way?

00:11:10.480 --> 00:11:12.730
This is an interesting
opportunity, too,

00:11:12.730 --> 00:11:14.650
to get ahead of some
change management needs

00:11:14.650 --> 00:11:16.540
related to adoption of ML.

00:11:16.540 --> 00:11:19.000
So this domain expert is
going to need to use this ML

00:11:19.000 --> 00:11:20.400
model down the road.

00:11:20.400 --> 00:11:22.510
And so engaging them
from the beginning,

00:11:22.510 --> 00:11:25.300
really making the most of
their institutional knowledge,

00:11:25.300 --> 00:11:27.220
will hopefully make
it easier for them

00:11:27.220 --> 00:11:29.790
once you're actually deploying
the model into production

00:11:29.790 --> 00:11:32.080
and you're asking
them to use it.

00:11:32.080 --> 00:11:35.290
The third role that's important
to consider is a data analyst.

00:11:35.290 --> 00:11:37.450
So we talked a lot about
the importance of data.

00:11:37.450 --> 00:11:39.970
We're going to talk more about
how you look at it as you're

00:11:39.970 --> 00:11:41.840
qualifying your ML use case.

00:11:41.840 --> 00:11:45.400
This person will be involved
in assessing that data,

00:11:45.400 --> 00:11:47.920
performing that
exploratory data analysis,

00:11:47.920 --> 00:11:51.720
trying to understand
things like data quality.

00:11:51.720 --> 00:11:55.040
Another role that's important
to include is the data engineer.

00:11:55.040 --> 00:11:56.540
And this person
is really involved

00:11:56.540 --> 00:11:58.610
in creating that data pipeline.

00:11:58.610 --> 00:12:00.830
And, again, the pipeline
is what is ultimately

00:12:00.830 --> 00:12:04.360
going to feed into your
machine learning model.

00:12:04.360 --> 00:12:07.120
A solution architect is
also another important role

00:12:07.120 --> 00:12:08.800
for an ML project.

00:12:08.800 --> 00:12:10.990
That person is going
to help you come up

00:12:10.990 --> 00:12:14.632
with your overarching solution
for deploying your ML model.

00:12:14.632 --> 00:12:16.090
You know, I mention
sometimes there

00:12:16.090 --> 00:12:18.255
are some technical
integrations that are needed.

00:12:18.255 --> 00:12:19.630
And that solution
architect would

00:12:19.630 --> 00:12:21.910
help define the
requirements and help

00:12:21.910 --> 00:12:24.190
to understand those
integrations so that you can

00:12:24.190 --> 00:12:26.530
have a successful deployment.

00:12:26.530 --> 00:12:29.390
And then the last role to keep
in mind is the project manager.

00:12:29.390 --> 00:12:32.000
So there's obviously a lot
of coordination involved

00:12:32.000 --> 00:12:35.590
across these different roles and
throughout the ML life cycle.

00:12:35.590 --> 00:12:38.000
And you need a project
manager to help orchestrate

00:12:38.000 --> 00:12:40.130
all those different activities.

00:12:40.130 --> 00:12:42.620
Now, it's often great
to think about someone

00:12:42.620 --> 00:12:46.216
who has some experience in
more of an agile methodology

00:12:46.216 --> 00:12:47.840
because, as I mentioned
before, there's

00:12:47.840 --> 00:12:50.120
a lot of iterations
and repeating

00:12:50.120 --> 00:12:52.040
that occurs in an ML project.

00:12:52.040 --> 00:12:54.890
So you want someone
who's got that experience

00:12:54.890 --> 00:12:57.980
and knows how to maintain
that flexibility,

00:12:57.980 --> 00:13:00.595
even as they're trying to
manage a more complex project.

00:13:03.580 --> 00:13:06.130
So I mentioned we're going to
focus on steps for use case

00:13:06.130 --> 00:13:07.300
identification.

00:13:07.300 --> 00:13:10.100
Here's the way we think
about it with our clients.

00:13:10.100 --> 00:13:13.270
So first, we want you to
identify and prioritize

00:13:13.270 --> 00:13:15.580
your top business challenges.

00:13:15.580 --> 00:13:17.920
This will help to ensure
that your ML projects have

00:13:17.920 --> 00:13:20.080
the greatest business impact.

00:13:20.080 --> 00:13:22.780
So oftentimes, we'll
get people together,

00:13:22.780 --> 00:13:25.540
and it'll be a mix
of stakeholders.

00:13:25.540 --> 00:13:29.650
And we'll have them come up with
those top business challenges.

00:13:29.650 --> 00:13:31.390
Following that,
once you've agreed

00:13:31.390 --> 00:13:33.670
on what those top
business challenges are,

00:13:33.670 --> 00:13:36.720
we want you to brainstorm
and get creative around ML

00:13:36.720 --> 00:13:40.390
use cases that address those
top business challenges.

00:13:40.390 --> 00:13:42.210
So sometimes, it's
thinking about what

00:13:42.210 --> 00:13:44.790
decisions we need to make
over and over again that could

00:13:44.790 --> 00:13:48.579
benefit from predictions.

00:13:48.579 --> 00:13:50.120
Getting people
together and coming up

00:13:50.120 --> 00:13:52.730
with a large quantity
of ML use cases

00:13:52.730 --> 00:13:54.110
is a great way to do this.

00:13:54.110 --> 00:13:56.210
And then, we often
have people vote

00:13:56.210 --> 00:14:00.280
collaboratively to decide where
do we think we should start.

00:14:00.280 --> 00:14:01.930
And that voting
helps us to come up

00:14:01.930 --> 00:14:04.390
with a short list
of ML use cases

00:14:04.390 --> 00:14:07.480
that we really do some
deeper qualification around.

00:14:07.480 --> 00:14:09.940
So normally, we look at
about three to five use cases

00:14:09.940 --> 00:14:12.790
that we'll hope to
put on our ML roadmap.

00:14:12.790 --> 00:14:15.920
And step three involves
qualifying those use cases.

00:14:15.920 --> 00:14:18.270
So we'll usually take a
deeper look at the data.

00:14:18.270 --> 00:14:20.270
We'll talk more about
what we do there to assess

00:14:20.270 --> 00:14:21.820
technical feasibility.

00:14:21.820 --> 00:14:25.030
It's also good practice
to really quantify

00:14:25.030 --> 00:14:27.246
the business impact
of the ML use cases,

00:14:27.246 --> 00:14:28.870
again, so you can
make sure that you're

00:14:28.870 --> 00:14:32.140
justifying your investment
in the ML project.

00:14:32.140 --> 00:14:35.946
After that, you can create your
ML roadmap for development.

00:14:35.946 --> 00:14:38.570
And we'll talk a little bit more
about how to do that later on.

00:14:41.410 --> 00:14:45.030
So as you're starting to think
about ML and where to begin,

00:14:45.030 --> 00:14:48.900
I want you to consider where
you are on this path to ML.

00:14:48.900 --> 00:14:50.970
And this path is
really the journey

00:14:50.970 --> 00:14:53.930
that any process or
product might go through

00:14:53.930 --> 00:14:55.470
as it's maturing.

00:14:55.470 --> 00:14:59.460
So I'm going to walk through
one example related to a contact

00:14:59.460 --> 00:15:01.470
center or a support
team, but you

00:15:01.470 --> 00:15:06.160
can think about any process
or product in the same way.

00:15:06.160 --> 00:15:10.170
So if you have a
new contact center,

00:15:10.170 --> 00:15:13.440
at first, you may receive
requests from customers.

00:15:13.440 --> 00:15:16.410
And you may only have one
person answering the phone.

00:15:16.410 --> 00:15:18.970
I know a lot of
startups begin this way.

00:15:18.970 --> 00:15:20.970
But over time, as you get
more and more requests

00:15:20.970 --> 00:15:24.390
from customers, you'll move out
of that individual contributor

00:15:24.390 --> 00:15:27.870
phase and into the next step,
which is around delegation.

00:15:27.870 --> 00:15:32.400
You might need a team of people
to respond to those customer

00:15:32.400 --> 00:15:34.170
support requests.

00:15:34.170 --> 00:15:37.020
And that is phase
two of delegation.

00:15:37.020 --> 00:15:39.510
During this phase, there will
be a lot of standardization

00:15:39.510 --> 00:15:41.550
that happens of your
business processes

00:15:41.550 --> 00:15:46.160
so that anyone can
actually perform that role.

00:15:46.160 --> 00:15:48.800
After that, we often
see customers move

00:15:48.800 --> 00:15:51.050
into this digitization phase.

00:15:51.050 --> 00:15:55.400
So this will be where you'll
maybe set up a ticketing system

00:15:55.400 --> 00:15:59.060
to automate and help
digitize those support

00:15:59.060 --> 00:16:02.390
requests, the
routing, the solution.

00:16:02.390 --> 00:16:04.940
And this is an important
step because it really

00:16:04.940 --> 00:16:08.540
liberates and starts to
collect a lot of the data

00:16:08.540 --> 00:16:10.760
that in the past you
maybe weren't capturing

00:16:10.760 --> 00:16:12.590
in any sort of meaningful way.

00:16:12.590 --> 00:16:15.110
And over time, as you
acquire more and more data,

00:16:15.110 --> 00:16:18.380
you move into this big
data and analytics phase.

00:16:18.380 --> 00:16:21.860
And sometimes, you might stay
in this phase, and that's OK.

00:16:21.860 --> 00:16:24.410
But sometimes, you may
want to do something

00:16:24.410 --> 00:16:26.570
more sophisticated, again,
especially when you're

00:16:26.570 --> 00:16:28.340
making repeated decisions.

00:16:28.340 --> 00:16:31.490
So you might want to move into
this machine learning phase.

00:16:31.490 --> 00:16:34.380
So with this contact
center example,

00:16:34.380 --> 00:16:38.210
you may move into a phase
where you start automatically

00:16:38.210 --> 00:16:41.990
routing and predicting
the types of issues

00:16:41.990 --> 00:16:44.840
that customers have
based on their messages

00:16:44.840 --> 00:16:46.234
that they're sending you.

00:16:46.234 --> 00:16:48.650
So that's a very common use
case we see a lot of companies

00:16:48.650 --> 00:16:49.670
implementing.

00:16:49.670 --> 00:16:51.320
But in order to get
there, you really

00:16:51.320 --> 00:16:54.110
have to make sure you're in that
big data and analytics phase,

00:16:54.110 --> 00:16:56.360
that you're collecting
that large volume of data

00:16:56.360 --> 00:17:00.100
that really is required for
any machine learning project.

00:17:00.100 --> 00:17:05.079
So, again, consider where
you are on this path to ML

00:17:05.079 --> 00:17:08.950
and how mature your business
process or product might be.

00:17:11.940 --> 00:17:13.859
As you're thinking
about ML projects,

00:17:13.859 --> 00:17:16.349
I also think it's good to be
realistic about the effort

00:17:16.349 --> 00:17:17.829
that's required.

00:17:17.829 --> 00:17:21.450
It's a little bit different
than your traditional software

00:17:21.450 --> 00:17:23.190
implementation projects.

00:17:23.190 --> 00:17:25.770
So think about these
four activities

00:17:25.770 --> 00:17:29.310
that are involved-- defining
key performance indicators,

00:17:29.310 --> 00:17:33.000
collecting data, building
infrastructure, and optimizing

00:17:33.000 --> 00:17:34.780
your ML algorithm.

00:17:34.780 --> 00:17:37.150
So a question for you--

00:17:37.150 --> 00:17:38.970
what percent of time
and effort do you

00:17:38.970 --> 00:17:40.910
think each of these steps take?

00:17:46.460 --> 00:17:48.860
So what we often
hear from customers

00:17:48.860 --> 00:17:53.030
is that their expectation is
very different than reality.

00:17:53.030 --> 00:17:57.260
So you can see here, a lot of
people assume that optimizing

00:17:57.260 --> 00:18:00.830
the ML algorithm-- that
big green bar on top--

00:18:00.830 --> 00:18:04.370
is what really requires
a lot of time and effort.

00:18:04.370 --> 00:18:06.920
But in practice, that's
actually not true.

00:18:06.920 --> 00:18:09.500
You can see the
reality at the bottom.

00:18:09.500 --> 00:18:11.510
The dark blue area
of collecting data

00:18:11.510 --> 00:18:14.090
really takes quite a bit
of time for ML projects,

00:18:14.090 --> 00:18:16.260
so you should expect that.

00:18:16.260 --> 00:18:19.250
The other area that often takes
a lot of time with ML projects

00:18:19.250 --> 00:18:20.800
is building infrastructure.

00:18:20.800 --> 00:18:23.960
And that's represented by
that yellow bar at the bottom.

00:18:23.960 --> 00:18:26.060
Now, Google Cloud
actually has done

00:18:26.060 --> 00:18:28.760
a lot of the infrastructure
building for you.

00:18:28.760 --> 00:18:30.890
So if you use some of
our managed services,

00:18:30.890 --> 00:18:32.690
like Cloud Machine
Learning Engine,

00:18:32.690 --> 00:18:36.510
we'll actually take care of
a lot of that effort for you.

00:18:36.510 --> 00:18:38.895
So keep in mind this
ML effort allocation

00:18:38.895 --> 00:18:41.264
as you're thinking
about your projects

00:18:41.264 --> 00:18:42.680
and as you're doing
your planning.

00:18:42.680 --> 00:18:44.804
You're really going to want
to invest a lot of time

00:18:44.804 --> 00:18:45.960
in that data collection.

00:18:45.960 --> 00:18:48.590
And I think you'll be surprised
how little time is actually

00:18:48.590 --> 00:18:52.125
spent on optimizing the
specific ML algorithm.

00:18:54.920 --> 00:18:57.430
So as I mentioned before,
data qualification

00:18:57.430 --> 00:19:00.050
is a really important
step as you're

00:19:00.050 --> 00:19:03.150
thinking about the feasibility
of your ML use cases.

00:19:03.150 --> 00:19:06.890
So this is the list of
steps or a checklist

00:19:06.890 --> 00:19:10.100
that you can use as you're
thinking about ML use cases

00:19:10.100 --> 00:19:12.530
and you're trying to
assess their feasibility.

00:19:12.530 --> 00:19:15.320
As I'm walking through
these, think about the data

00:19:15.320 --> 00:19:18.110
that you have to support
your ML use cases

00:19:18.110 --> 00:19:19.920
and how it might measure up.

00:19:19.920 --> 00:19:23.540
So the very first step is
around assessing the feasibility

00:19:23.540 --> 00:19:27.390
based on any data ownership
or practical considerations.

00:19:27.390 --> 00:19:29.390
So, again, machine
learning projects

00:19:29.390 --> 00:19:31.460
require large amounts of data.

00:19:31.460 --> 00:19:34.250
And you want to make sure that
you have ownership of that data

00:19:34.250 --> 00:19:37.860
so that you can use it
to build your ML model.

00:19:37.860 --> 00:19:40.450
In many cases,
that's not an issue.

00:19:40.450 --> 00:19:42.372
So for sales
forecasting, for example,

00:19:42.372 --> 00:19:43.830
you probably own
a lot of that data

00:19:43.830 --> 00:19:47.460
yourself if you're
managing the stores

00:19:47.460 --> 00:19:48.854
where the sales are being made.

00:19:48.854 --> 00:19:50.520
But sometimes, there
are other scenarios

00:19:50.520 --> 00:19:52.050
where you don't own the data.

00:19:52.050 --> 00:19:54.450
And that can sometimes
be a blocker.

00:19:54.450 --> 00:19:57.700
So it's important to think about
those practical considerations.

00:19:57.700 --> 00:20:00.330
The next step is around
collecting samples.

00:20:00.330 --> 00:20:02.800
So once you've identified
your ML use case,

00:20:02.800 --> 00:20:05.040
we want you to consider
all of the data

00:20:05.040 --> 00:20:06.900
that you currently
collect that might

00:20:06.900 --> 00:20:10.670
be used in an ML prediction.

00:20:10.670 --> 00:20:13.770
So for the sales
forecasting example,

00:20:13.770 --> 00:20:16.100
you may have historical
sales records

00:20:16.100 --> 00:20:17.660
from all of your stores.

00:20:17.660 --> 00:20:19.580
And you'll want to get
a sample of that data

00:20:19.580 --> 00:20:22.890
as you're assessing the
feasibility of that use case.

00:20:22.890 --> 00:20:25.230
You may also want to bring
in other data sets, though--

00:20:25.230 --> 00:20:27.000
things like promotional data--

00:20:27.000 --> 00:20:30.880
that have a really strong
impact on the problem as well.

00:20:30.880 --> 00:20:33.060
So identify all those
different data sets.

00:20:33.060 --> 00:20:35.460
And then collect the samples.

00:20:35.460 --> 00:20:36.960
You can then hand
over those samples

00:20:36.960 --> 00:20:39.120
to your data analyst who
will take a look at things

00:20:39.120 --> 00:20:40.800
like data quality.

00:20:40.800 --> 00:20:43.560
When I say data quality,
we're looking at things

00:20:43.560 --> 00:20:46.080
like are there a lot
of missing values.

00:20:46.080 --> 00:20:50.340
Is there a lot of constant
values in the data set itself?

00:20:50.340 --> 00:20:53.880
That can really help us to
understand if the data is

00:20:53.880 --> 00:20:56.170
good quality or not.

00:20:56.170 --> 00:20:58.830
You also want to ensure
you have labeled data.

00:20:58.830 --> 00:21:01.500
And this may be a new
concept for some of you,

00:21:01.500 --> 00:21:05.070
but it's very critical for
machine learning projects.

00:21:05.070 --> 00:21:06.720
So when I say labeled
data, you want

00:21:06.720 --> 00:21:12.160
to have historical examples of
what you're trying to predict.

00:21:12.160 --> 00:21:14.520
So, for example, with
sales forecasting,

00:21:14.520 --> 00:21:16.470
you want those
historical sales records.

00:21:16.470 --> 00:21:18.720
And if you're trying to
predict sales volume,

00:21:18.720 --> 00:21:21.420
you need to understand
what that sales

00:21:21.420 --> 00:21:23.850
volume has been in the past.

00:21:23.850 --> 00:21:26.950
That data is used to train
the ML model to teach it.

00:21:26.950 --> 00:21:29.640
So if you don't have
those labeled examples,

00:21:29.640 --> 00:21:33.400
you won't have a way
to train your ML model.

00:21:33.400 --> 00:21:36.010
You also want to verify that
your data sets are large enough

00:21:36.010 --> 00:21:37.342
for model building.

00:21:37.342 --> 00:21:39.550
So one of the reasons why
machine learning is gaining

00:21:39.550 --> 00:21:43.870
so much steam these days
is because it has a lot

00:21:43.870 --> 00:21:47.230
to do with all that big
data that's being collected

00:21:47.230 --> 00:21:49.105
in many different industries.

00:21:49.105 --> 00:21:50.480
So you want to
make sure, though,

00:21:50.480 --> 00:21:52.720
that you have a
large enough volume

00:21:52.720 --> 00:21:55.810
to perform well in these
machine learning projects.

00:21:55.810 --> 00:21:58.180
Generally, the more data
you have, the better.

00:21:58.180 --> 00:22:01.600
But when we're looking at
some of these deep learning

00:22:01.600 --> 00:22:03.700
algorithms, the more
complex algorithms that

00:22:03.700 --> 00:22:05.770
are based on the way
your brain works,

00:22:05.770 --> 00:22:08.200
we often look for
data points that

00:22:08.200 --> 00:22:10.690
are maybe in the hundreds of
thousands as a minimum to get

00:22:10.690 --> 00:22:11.410
started.

00:22:11.410 --> 00:22:13.330
So, again, you want
to take a closer look

00:22:13.330 --> 00:22:14.890
at the data you
have and make sure

00:22:14.890 --> 00:22:16.990
that you have a large
enough volume of data.

00:22:16.990 --> 00:22:20.020
And lastly, you want to
check for class imbalance.

00:22:20.020 --> 00:22:21.640
What is class imbalance?

00:22:21.640 --> 00:22:23.180
Well, it just means
you need to have

00:22:23.180 --> 00:22:26.410
enough positive and negative
examples or enough examples

00:22:26.410 --> 00:22:29.050
of different categories that
relate to your machine learning

00:22:29.050 --> 00:22:30.050
problem.

00:22:30.050 --> 00:22:32.470
So, for example, I
worked with one customer

00:22:32.470 --> 00:22:34.900
that was trying to
predict truck breakdowns.

00:22:34.900 --> 00:22:38.650
And we needed to have
more examples of trucks

00:22:38.650 --> 00:22:40.330
that had actually broken down.

00:22:40.330 --> 00:22:42.730
In the initial data set,
they were collecting

00:22:42.730 --> 00:22:43.950
a lot of data points.

00:22:43.950 --> 00:22:47.200
But they only had 32 examples
of trucks that had broken down.

00:22:47.200 --> 00:22:50.350
And that's an example
of that class imbalance.

00:22:50.350 --> 00:22:53.200
You want to make sure you have
enough historical examples

00:22:53.200 --> 00:22:55.390
of those trucks that
are functioning well

00:22:55.390 --> 00:22:57.590
and the ones that
have broken down.

00:22:57.590 --> 00:22:59.740
So, again, you can use
this as a checklist

00:22:59.740 --> 00:23:02.650
to think about how does
your data measure up

00:23:02.650 --> 00:23:04.120
for your machine
learning project.

00:23:07.490 --> 00:23:11.210
So in this next slide, this
is an example of an output

00:23:11.210 --> 00:23:13.910
that you might have as you're
going through this use case

00:23:13.910 --> 00:23:15.900
identification process.

00:23:15.900 --> 00:23:20.490
So on the left, you can see four
use cases that were identified.

00:23:20.490 --> 00:23:22.640
So imagine we just
had a great workshop

00:23:22.640 --> 00:23:25.040
with different stakeholders
across the business

00:23:25.040 --> 00:23:27.500
and from our tech organization.

00:23:27.500 --> 00:23:30.650
And we came up with
these four use cases

00:23:30.650 --> 00:23:33.530
as a way of solving our
top business problem--

00:23:33.530 --> 00:23:37.010
so things like cross sell, up
sell, customer-centric store

00:23:37.010 --> 00:23:40.250
layouts, a recommendation
engine for online ordering,

00:23:40.250 --> 00:23:43.760
and a chat bot to
support online orders.

00:23:43.760 --> 00:23:45.890
So through our
qualification process,

00:23:45.890 --> 00:23:48.380
we assessed the business impact
and technical feasibility

00:23:48.380 --> 00:23:49.460
of each use case.

00:23:49.460 --> 00:23:51.650
And on the right, you
see a nice visual summary

00:23:51.650 --> 00:23:54.750
of that in this 2 by 2 matrix.

00:23:54.750 --> 00:23:56.630
So you can see here,
some of these use cases

00:23:56.630 --> 00:23:58.580
have a much higher
business impact,

00:23:58.580 --> 00:24:00.230
like cross sell, up sell.

00:24:00.230 --> 00:24:03.590
That definitely would have a
nice tie to increasing revenue.

00:24:03.590 --> 00:24:05.840
But it's not quite as
feasible based on the data

00:24:05.840 --> 00:24:08.780
that we looked at and the data
that we're collecting today.

00:24:08.780 --> 00:24:11.840
So for our ML project, we
may want to actually begin

00:24:11.840 --> 00:24:13.700
with use case three or four.

00:24:13.700 --> 00:24:16.160
These still have a pretty
high business impact,

00:24:16.160 --> 00:24:18.820
and they're much more usable.

00:24:18.820 --> 00:24:21.720
So a recommendation engine for
online ordering or a chat bot

00:24:21.720 --> 00:24:24.690
to support online orders
could be two places

00:24:24.690 --> 00:24:25.590
where we could start.

00:24:28.220 --> 00:24:30.850
Now I'm going to walk through
a few case studies that

00:24:30.850 --> 00:24:34.880
show you how other businesses
are applying machine learning.

00:24:34.880 --> 00:24:37.130
So Uniqlo is a
Japanese retailer that

00:24:37.130 --> 00:24:41.300
operates close to 2,000
stores all around the world.

00:24:41.300 --> 00:24:44.150
And they wanted to find ways
to make their customer shopping

00:24:44.150 --> 00:24:47.790
experience easier
and more enjoyable.

00:24:47.790 --> 00:24:50.940
They looked at some of their
top business challenges,

00:24:50.940 --> 00:24:53.430
and those involved
things like the changing

00:24:53.430 --> 00:24:54.910
retail environment.

00:24:54.910 --> 00:24:59.100
We all know that the way that
we make purchases and we shop

00:24:59.100 --> 00:25:01.500
is really changing quite a bit.

00:25:01.500 --> 00:25:04.260
And they also wanted ways
to bridge the offline

00:25:04.260 --> 00:25:06.280
and online shopping experience.

00:25:06.280 --> 00:25:09.090
Uniqlo not only has many
stores around the world,

00:25:09.090 --> 00:25:11.430
but they also have a pretty
awesome website where

00:25:11.430 --> 00:25:13.450
you can buy things online.

00:25:13.450 --> 00:25:16.570
But they didn't have a good
way to link those things.

00:25:16.570 --> 00:25:18.690
So in terms of the
business impact,

00:25:18.690 --> 00:25:21.690
they really saw some
pretty impressive results.

00:25:21.690 --> 00:25:24.540
So they created a chat
bot that was integrated

00:25:24.540 --> 00:25:26.370
with their mobile app.

00:25:26.370 --> 00:25:28.170
And this chat bot
would provide answers

00:25:28.170 --> 00:25:30.390
to common customer questions.

00:25:30.390 --> 00:25:34.570
And these things ranged
from is this black sweater

00:25:34.570 --> 00:25:38.640
available in the store that's
nearest me to what color

00:25:38.640 --> 00:25:42.930
skirts do you have
available in this style.

00:25:42.930 --> 00:25:46.490
And they really saw a great
improvement in engagement

00:25:46.490 --> 00:25:49.550
as a result from this chat bot.

00:25:49.550 --> 00:25:54.210
So of the users that
had the app, 40% of them

00:25:54.210 --> 00:25:56.600
interacted with the chat
bot on a weekly basis--

00:25:56.600 --> 00:25:57.200
40%.

00:25:57.200 --> 00:26:01.480
So that's pretty impressive in
terms of engagement numbers.

00:26:01.480 --> 00:26:03.900
So when you think
about the chat bot,

00:26:03.900 --> 00:26:05.460
there's also some
great tools that we

00:26:05.460 --> 00:26:07.680
have to accelerate development.

00:26:07.680 --> 00:26:10.860
And so there's a great product
available called Dialogue

00:26:10.860 --> 00:26:13.050
Flow-- this is available
through Google--

00:26:13.050 --> 00:26:17.454
that really can make building
a chat bot faster and easier.

00:26:17.454 --> 00:26:18.870
So if this use
case interests you,

00:26:18.870 --> 00:26:20.578
I'd encourage you to
take a look at that.

00:26:22.830 --> 00:26:25.710
This next case study is
around sales forecasting.

00:26:25.710 --> 00:26:29.220
So we worked with a global
consumer products company,

00:26:29.220 --> 00:26:31.290
and they were interested
in improving their sales

00:26:31.290 --> 00:26:34.020
forecasts from
their distributors

00:26:34.020 --> 00:26:35.744
to their retailers.

00:26:35.744 --> 00:26:37.410
And there were a few
business challenges

00:26:37.410 --> 00:26:39.510
that were identified that
they were hoping machine

00:26:39.510 --> 00:26:41.170
learning would help them with.

00:26:41.170 --> 00:26:45.720
So their existing process for
forecasting was very manual,

00:26:45.720 --> 00:26:46.800
and it was very clunky.

00:26:46.800 --> 00:26:50.400
So they were using a lot
of random spreadsheets

00:26:50.400 --> 00:26:52.710
to generate forecasts.

00:26:52.710 --> 00:26:54.270
They were also
collecting quite a bit

00:26:54.270 --> 00:26:56.460
of data across
their organization,

00:26:56.460 --> 00:26:58.120
but they weren't
really using it.

00:26:58.120 --> 00:27:01.380
So they knew that they could
do better if they found ways

00:27:01.380 --> 00:27:03.990
to harness some of this data
that they were collecting

00:27:03.990 --> 00:27:04.920
today.

00:27:04.920 --> 00:27:07.560
But they were really only
using a few data sets

00:27:07.560 --> 00:27:09.960
around historical
sales and things

00:27:09.960 --> 00:27:13.752
like important holidays
and events and seasonality.

00:27:13.752 --> 00:27:15.210
So they also felt
they were missing

00:27:15.210 --> 00:27:16.800
some really important
signals that

00:27:16.800 --> 00:27:18.690
were relevant to the problem.

00:27:18.690 --> 00:27:21.190
So they had a hunch that
weather, for example,

00:27:21.190 --> 00:27:24.690
was very important in terms
of driving sales of some

00:27:24.690 --> 00:27:25.510
of their products.

00:27:25.510 --> 00:27:27.635
But till today, they didn't
really incorporate that

00:27:27.635 --> 00:27:29.520
into their modeling.

00:27:29.520 --> 00:27:33.060
So we worked with them to
create a custom ML model.

00:27:33.060 --> 00:27:36.840
And the model actually
yielded a 15% improvement

00:27:36.840 --> 00:27:38.850
in forecast accuracy.

00:27:38.850 --> 00:27:41.910
And it was projected to
save them about $2 million--

00:27:41.910 --> 00:27:44.550
so a pretty significant
business impact.

00:27:44.550 --> 00:27:47.160
And this model was
interesting for them

00:27:47.160 --> 00:27:50.070
because it incorporated quite a
few nontraditional data points,

00:27:50.070 --> 00:27:52.710
like weather, demographic
information, and even

00:27:52.710 --> 00:27:54.810
Google Trends information.

00:27:54.810 --> 00:27:58.290
So machine learning is a great
way for you to think about some

00:27:58.290 --> 00:28:00.570
of these external
data sets that might

00:28:00.570 --> 00:28:02.820
be relevant to your
business problem

00:28:02.820 --> 00:28:05.320
and really find ways
to incorporate them.

00:28:05.320 --> 00:28:07.230
So this company, for
example, had been doing

00:28:07.230 --> 00:28:09.060
a lot of statistical modeling.

00:28:09.060 --> 00:28:11.490
And they were doing some
pretty sophisticated stuff,

00:28:11.490 --> 00:28:13.200
but they weren't able
to incorporate some

00:28:13.200 --> 00:28:14.810
of these other data points.

00:28:14.810 --> 00:28:16.510
And with ML, they
were able to do that.

00:28:19.510 --> 00:28:22.780
So I'll walk through the top
five tips that I have for you

00:28:22.780 --> 00:28:25.250
before you start
your ML project.

00:28:25.250 --> 00:28:27.880
So the first is around making
sure your use cases have

00:28:27.880 --> 00:28:29.380
a high business impact.

00:28:29.380 --> 00:28:32.440
Again, we want to make sure you
get the return on investment

00:28:32.440 --> 00:28:34.960
for your ML project,
especially if you're just

00:28:34.960 --> 00:28:36.790
getting started in this area.

00:28:36.790 --> 00:28:40.780
So really quantify the revenue
and cost savings associated

00:28:40.780 --> 00:28:43.190
with a specific ML project.

00:28:43.190 --> 00:28:46.610
Get your hands around your
data to assess feasibility.

00:28:46.610 --> 00:28:48.520
We walked through
that data checklist,

00:28:48.520 --> 00:28:51.470
and it's very important that
you go through that for your ML

00:28:51.470 --> 00:28:52.860
projects.

00:28:52.860 --> 00:28:54.980
We also encourage you
to get samples of data

00:28:54.980 --> 00:28:57.950
so you can really take a
closer look at data quality

00:28:57.950 --> 00:29:00.890
and make sure that the
use case is feasible

00:29:00.890 --> 00:29:05.670
based on all of that data that
you're already collecting.

00:29:05.670 --> 00:29:07.380
Number three is
around being clear

00:29:07.380 --> 00:29:09.810
about what decisions
you'll make with ML.

00:29:09.810 --> 00:29:11.690
So remember back
to the beginning.

00:29:11.690 --> 00:29:14.160
ML is all about
making predictions

00:29:14.160 --> 00:29:16.840
that can help you make
better business decisions.

00:29:16.840 --> 00:29:18.840
So think about what
those decisions are.

00:29:18.840 --> 00:29:20.940
What does that process
look like today?

00:29:20.940 --> 00:29:26.270
And then how can ML help make
those business decisions easier

00:29:26.270 --> 00:29:29.974
or better or more accurate?

00:29:29.974 --> 00:29:31.390
Once you've done
those things, you

00:29:31.390 --> 00:29:34.020
should be able to
build a roadmap for ML.

00:29:34.020 --> 00:29:36.700
And we encourage you to
start with quick wins.

00:29:36.700 --> 00:29:39.490
What we've found is
that over time, you'll

00:29:39.490 --> 00:29:43.540
adopt ML for one you
case, and then you'll

00:29:43.540 --> 00:29:46.030
adopt it for maybe two
or three and that you'll

00:29:46.030 --> 00:29:49.220
have an acceleration of using
ML in your organization.

00:29:49.220 --> 00:29:51.550
It's actually what we've
seen at Google over time

00:29:51.550 --> 00:29:55.450
as we've done more and more
ML within our products.

00:29:55.450 --> 00:29:59.110
But in order to get started and
really generate some results,

00:29:59.110 --> 00:30:01.090
we encourage you to
start with quick wins.

00:30:01.090 --> 00:30:05.590
Going through that process will
help you to establish a team,

00:30:05.590 --> 00:30:07.900
establish a model,
a way of working,

00:30:07.900 --> 00:30:11.770
and also improve the quality
of your data for that use case

00:30:11.770 --> 00:30:14.834
but even for another
ML use case as well.

00:30:14.834 --> 00:30:17.000
And then finally, the last
thing I'll leave you with

00:30:17.000 --> 00:30:18.790
is, remember, it's math.

00:30:18.790 --> 00:30:22.110
It's not magic.

00:30:22.110 --> 00:30:25.037
So stay tuned for
our live Q&amp;A. We'll

00:30:25.037 --> 00:30:26.120
be back in about a minute.

00:32:13.700 --> 00:32:14.200
All right.

00:32:14.200 --> 00:32:15.880
So we've got some
great questions

00:32:15.880 --> 00:32:19.080
from our audience related
to starting ML projects.

00:32:19.080 --> 00:32:22.930
So the first is "how is ML
different than big data?"

00:32:22.930 --> 00:32:24.250
That's a great question.

00:32:24.250 --> 00:32:26.860
We talked a lot about how
it's important to really

00:32:26.860 --> 00:32:29.740
be at that big data
stage before you

00:32:29.740 --> 00:32:31.990
can move into an ML project.

00:32:31.990 --> 00:32:33.960
When you think about the
difference between ML

00:32:33.960 --> 00:32:36.820
and big data, remember,
ML is about making

00:32:36.820 --> 00:32:40.210
those forward-looking
predictions.

00:32:40.210 --> 00:32:42.430
With big data,
you're really looking

00:32:42.430 --> 00:32:44.770
at historical information.

00:32:44.770 --> 00:32:47.020
And you're performing
analysis based

00:32:47.020 --> 00:32:49.720
on those historical trends.

00:32:49.720 --> 00:32:53.620
So big data I like to
think of and associate

00:32:53.620 --> 00:32:56.650
with a live, real-time
dashboard where you're seeing

00:32:56.650 --> 00:32:58.930
exactly what has just happened.

00:32:58.930 --> 00:33:01.600
But, again, machine
learning is actually

00:33:01.600 --> 00:33:05.270
all about thinking of
forward-looking predictions.

00:33:05.270 --> 00:33:08.350
So, for example, big
data could tell you

00:33:08.350 --> 00:33:13.930
what your sales were and trends
in sales for the past month.

00:33:13.930 --> 00:33:17.680
But ML could tell you what
do we believe the sales will

00:33:17.680 --> 00:33:20.840
be for next month.

00:33:20.840 --> 00:33:21.340
OK.

00:33:21.340 --> 00:33:23.680
The next question-- "who
needs to be involved

00:33:23.680 --> 00:33:26.330
in defining ML use cases?"

00:33:26.330 --> 00:33:28.570
So this is another
really important point

00:33:28.570 --> 00:33:30.700
that I want to make
sure you understand.

00:33:30.700 --> 00:33:33.790
So ML use case
definition is really

00:33:33.790 --> 00:33:36.310
a cross-functional
collaboration.

00:33:36.310 --> 00:33:39.280
You need to bring in
people from your business.

00:33:39.280 --> 00:33:41.860
You need to bring in
your data experts.

00:33:41.860 --> 00:33:44.200
You want to bring in
your technology leaders

00:33:44.200 --> 00:33:46.090
and your business sponsors.

00:33:46.090 --> 00:33:50.050
So you really want to make sure
you have that diverse team.

00:33:50.050 --> 00:33:51.880
There's also that
brainstorming step

00:33:51.880 --> 00:33:55.490
that's involved with
defining the ML use cases.

00:33:55.490 --> 00:33:58.330
And what we find is when you
have a more diverse team,

00:33:58.330 --> 00:34:02.770
you'll often come up with many
more ideas and better ideas

00:34:02.770 --> 00:34:05.510
at the end of that
brainstorming session.

00:34:05.510 --> 00:34:08.440
So remember, you want to have
that very diverse team of folks

00:34:08.440 --> 00:34:10.060
across your organization.

00:34:10.060 --> 00:34:13.120
And we encourage you to think
about business and technology

00:34:13.120 --> 00:34:15.040
and also different
levels-- so everyone

00:34:15.040 --> 00:34:18.820
from maybe your senior
leaders, your directors, all

00:34:18.820 --> 00:34:20.739
the way down to
that data analyst

00:34:20.739 --> 00:34:22.510
who knows the data
really well and maybe

00:34:22.510 --> 00:34:25.670
is really excited
about machine learning.

00:34:25.670 --> 00:34:28.800
"How much data do you
need for an ML project?"

00:34:28.800 --> 00:34:30.739
So this is another
good question.

00:34:30.739 --> 00:34:33.770
We talked a lot about
how ML is really powered

00:34:33.770 --> 00:34:36.330
by large quantities of data.

00:34:36.330 --> 00:34:39.010
And in general, when we're
thinking about a deep learning

00:34:39.010 --> 00:34:41.510
algorithms, a good
rule of thumb is

00:34:41.510 --> 00:34:44.449
to think about having hundreds
of thousands of data points

00:34:44.449 --> 00:34:45.840
as a starting point.

00:34:45.840 --> 00:34:47.540
Now, there are some
nuances to that

00:34:47.540 --> 00:34:51.449
that you may need to consider
for specific ML problems.

00:34:51.449 --> 00:34:54.110
And that's where an ML
engineer can help as you're

00:34:54.110 --> 00:34:55.820
thinking about those things.

00:34:55.820 --> 00:34:59.210
But, again, we think that large
quantity of data is important.

00:34:59.210 --> 00:35:02.070
What we also find is
the more data you have,

00:35:02.070 --> 00:35:04.370
the better your ML
model will perform.

00:35:04.370 --> 00:35:07.700
So, again, if you have
some pretty high bars

00:35:07.700 --> 00:35:10.260
for your performance
of your ML model,

00:35:10.260 --> 00:35:13.520
you really do want to
collect a lot of data--

00:35:13.520 --> 00:35:15.260
as much data as possible.

00:35:15.260 --> 00:35:18.530
That will really improve
that model's performance.

00:35:18.530 --> 00:35:19.030
OK.

00:35:19.030 --> 00:35:20.500
And the next question--

00:35:20.500 --> 00:35:25.210
"how do you assess the business
impact of an ML use case?"

00:35:25.210 --> 00:35:27.700
So for this one, we
talked a little bit

00:35:27.700 --> 00:35:31.090
about two important ways of
looking at the ML use case.

00:35:31.090 --> 00:35:33.470
You want to look at
the impact on revenue.

00:35:33.470 --> 00:35:36.400
So is there a way that this ML
use case might substantially

00:35:36.400 --> 00:35:38.260
increase revenue?

00:35:38.260 --> 00:35:41.860
Or is there a way that
it will save costs

00:35:41.860 --> 00:35:43.570
that you might have today?

00:35:43.570 --> 00:35:46.930
So I would think about
those two things.

00:35:46.930 --> 00:35:53.230
But in your organization, you
may have some standard ways

00:35:53.230 --> 00:35:55.690
that you approach
creating a business case.

00:35:55.690 --> 00:35:59.290
And ML is not any different
than other business cases

00:35:59.290 --> 00:36:01.450
that you may have put
together in the past.

00:36:01.450 --> 00:36:05.740
So you can also use any
sort of process or criteria

00:36:05.740 --> 00:36:08.080
that you may have in your
company today when you're

00:36:08.080 --> 00:36:10.930
building business cases
around other projects

00:36:10.930 --> 00:36:13.144
or other investments.

00:36:13.144 --> 00:36:15.560
So keep that in mind as you're
thinking about the business

00:36:15.560 --> 00:36:18.200
impact of those ML use cases.

00:36:18.200 --> 00:36:18.700
All right.

00:36:18.700 --> 00:36:20.740
And then the last
question we have here

00:36:20.740 --> 00:36:23.560
is "what are some
other examples of using

00:36:23.560 --> 00:36:25.610
machine learning in retail?"

00:36:25.610 --> 00:36:26.110
OK.

00:36:26.110 --> 00:36:28.150
This is a really good one, too.

00:36:28.150 --> 00:36:32.740
I think retail is a space
where there's a lot of ways

00:36:32.740 --> 00:36:35.660
that ML can really
make a big impact.

00:36:35.660 --> 00:36:39.160
So a few big high-level
categories to think about

00:36:39.160 --> 00:36:44.110
are things like merchandising,
supply chain, pricing,

00:36:44.110 --> 00:36:48.490
customer experience, as
well as digital insights.

00:36:48.490 --> 00:36:51.790
So when I think about
those five different areas,

00:36:51.790 --> 00:36:54.280
when we think about
things like merchandising,

00:36:54.280 --> 00:36:56.530
how does ML play a role there?

00:36:56.530 --> 00:36:58.510
Well, you can look at
things like predicting

00:36:58.510 --> 00:37:01.690
trends that may affect
the stock that you

00:37:01.690 --> 00:37:03.820
want to have in your stores.

00:37:03.820 --> 00:37:05.560
Supply chain is
another great area

00:37:05.560 --> 00:37:07.700
where ML can make a big impact.

00:37:07.700 --> 00:37:11.590
So you can think about different
places in your supply chain

00:37:11.590 --> 00:37:13.750
where you might want
to make predictions

00:37:13.750 --> 00:37:15.130
that can optimize it--

00:37:15.130 --> 00:37:17.740
so everything from
how do we want

00:37:17.740 --> 00:37:21.850
to set our production sizes, to
what is our minimum inventory

00:37:21.850 --> 00:37:24.100
level in the store,
to how can we

00:37:24.100 --> 00:37:27.220
keep our shelves from
stocking out of items

00:37:27.220 --> 00:37:29.200
that are in high demand.

00:37:29.200 --> 00:37:31.770
I also mentioned the
customer experience.

00:37:31.770 --> 00:37:34.080
There are a lot of ways
that ML can help there.

00:37:34.080 --> 00:37:37.090
So you saw the one example
of a chat bot from Uniqlo.

00:37:37.090 --> 00:37:39.010
But you can think of
other ways that ML

00:37:39.010 --> 00:37:42.280
can improve the customer
experience, so things

00:37:42.280 --> 00:37:44.200
like a recommendation engine.

00:37:44.200 --> 00:37:46.750
This is a really great
quick win for a lot

00:37:46.750 --> 00:37:50.620
of retail organizations-- so
looking maybe at your website

00:37:50.620 --> 00:37:54.340
and looking at ways that you can
recommend products to customers

00:37:54.340 --> 00:37:55.810
based on the data
that you may have

00:37:55.810 --> 00:38:01.020
collected about their
shopping behavior in the past.

00:38:01.020 --> 00:38:04.200
So the last area mentioned was
around the digital experience

00:38:04.200 --> 00:38:05.430
and digital insights.

00:38:05.430 --> 00:38:07.321
And, again, for those
online storefronts

00:38:07.321 --> 00:38:08.820
that you might have,
you're probably

00:38:08.820 --> 00:38:10.380
collecting a lot of data.

00:38:10.380 --> 00:38:12.030
And you can think
about different ways

00:38:12.030 --> 00:38:14.947
you can optimize that
experience for your customer

00:38:14.947 --> 00:38:17.280
as they are shopping and
trying to make those purchasing

00:38:17.280 --> 00:38:19.270
decisions.

00:38:19.270 --> 00:38:20.140
All right.

00:38:20.140 --> 00:38:24.340
So I think that wraps up all
of our questions for today.

00:38:24.340 --> 00:38:27.070
So stay tuned for
the next session,

00:38:27.070 --> 00:38:30.220
accelerate cloud adoption
using API management

00:38:30.220 --> 00:38:32.530
as an abstraction layer.

00:38:32.530 --> 00:38:35.580
[MUSIC PLAYING]

